<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Changing the Subject 1 - Linear formula</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the formula \(y = mx + c\), where \(m \neq 0\).</p>
<p>(a) Make \(x\) the subject of the formula.</p>
<div>\(x = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Use your answer to part (a) to find the value of \(x\) when \(y = {@py@}\), \(m = {@pm@}\) and \(c = {@pc@}\).</p>
<div>\(x = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<h4>(a) Make \(x\) the subject</h4>
<p><strong>Step 1: subtract \(c\) from both sides.</strong> \(c\) is added to \(mx\), so we subtract \(c\) to remove it from the right-hand side:</p>
\[ y - c = mx + c - c \quad\Longrightarrow\quad y - c = mx. \]
<p><strong>Step 2: divide both sides by \(m\).</strong> \(x\) is multiplied by \(m\), so we divide by \(m\), which is allowed because \(m \neq 0\). The <em>whole</em> left-hand side \(y - c\) is divided by \(m\):</p>
\[ \frac{y - c}{m} = \frac{mx}{m} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \frac{y - c}{m} = x. \]
<p><strong>Step 3: write \(x\) on the left.</strong> Swapping the two sides does not change the equation:</p>
\[ x = \frac{y - c}{m}. \]
<p>A common mistake is to <em>add</em> \(c\) in Step 1, giving \(x = \frac{y + c}{m}\). To remove a term that is added, you must subtract it.</p>
<h4>(b) Find \(x\) when \(y = {@py@}\), \(m = {@pm@}\) and \(c = {@pc@}\)</h4>
<p><strong>Step 1: substitute the values into the formula from part (a).</strong></p>
\[ x = \frac{y - c}{m} = \frac{ {@py@} - {@pc@} }{ {@pm@} }. \]
<p><strong>Step 2: work out the numerator.</strong> \({@py@} - {@pc@} = {@py-pc@}\), so</p>
\[ x = \frac{ {@py-pc@} }{ {@pm@} }. \]
<p><strong>Step 3: divide.</strong></p>
\[ x = {@px@}. \]]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
